WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.697
Every day, the Sun seems
to move across the sky.

2
00:00:07.917 --> 00:00:11.679
Where does it rise and set?
And what happens to shadows?

3
00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:15.569
In the morning, the Sun
comes up low in the east.

4
00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:17.972
A stick in the ground makes a shadow.

5
00:00:18.192 --> 00:00:21.860
A shadow is the dark patch where
the stick blocks the light.

6
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.235
The morning shadow is long. It stretches
across the ground, pointing west.

7
00:00:27.455 --> 00:00:31.263
It points away from the Sun, on
the opposite side of the stick.

8
00:00:31.763 --> 00:00:34.875
All morning, the Sun
climbs higher in the sky.

9
00:00:35.095 --> 00:00:38.438
By midday, it has climbed
to its highest point.

10
00:00:38.658 --> 00:00:42.095
Now the shadow is short.
It is the shortest all day.

11
00:00:42.315 --> 00:00:44.823
The higher the Sun,
the shorter the shadow.

12
00:00:45.323 --> 00:00:48.620
In the afternoon, the Sun
sinks towards the west.

13
00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.998
Shadows grow long again.
Now they point east.

14
00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:55.887
In the evening, it sinks low
in the west and disappears.

15
00:00:56.107 --> 00:00:59.590
The Sun rises in the east and
sets in the west, every day.

16
00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:04.409
Never look straight at the Sun. Look
at the shadow on the ground instead.

17
00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:10.016
Stand a stick in the sun. Measure its
shadow at nine, at midday and at three.

18
00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:14.972
Lay hand spans along it, end to end.
Start at the bottom of the stick.

19
00:01:15.192 --> 00:01:20.951
At nine, it is three hands long. At
midday, one hand. At three, three hands.

20
00:01:21.451 --> 00:01:24.748
Write each time and its
shadow's length in a table.

21
00:01:24.968 --> 00:01:28.823
Then make a block graph, with
one block for each hand span.

22
00:01:29.043 --> 00:01:34.708
Long, short, long. The shadow gets
shorter towards midday, then longer again.

23
00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:39.944
The columns go down, then up again. The
pattern is decreasing, then increasing.

24
00:01:40.164 --> 00:01:43.647
The shortest shadow marks
the highest Sun, at midday.

25
00:01:44.147 --> 00:01:47.816
It looks as if the Sun moves
across the sky. It does not.

26
00:01:48.036 --> 00:01:52.262
The Earth turns, once a day.
That swings us past the Sun.

27
00:01:52.482 --> 00:01:57.358
On a roundabout, the playground seems
to move. It is you that is turning.

28
00:01:57.578 --> 00:02:00.643
Long ago, people thought the
Sun went round the Earth.

29
00:02:00.863 --> 00:02:07.132
Astronomers are scientists who study the sky.
They worked it out by watching and measuring.

30
00:02:07.632 --> 00:02:12.880
The Sun rises in the east, is highest
at midday, and sets in the west.

31
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:16.583
Shadows are long, then
short, then long again.

32
00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:22.051
You can measure a shadow in hand spans.
Then record it in a table and a graph.

33
00:02:22.271 --> 00:02:25.011
And it is the Earth
that turns, not the Sun.
